\begin_layout Section
Memory rules
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The memory game,
 also known as Concentration,
 is a simple card-matching game played by one or more players.
 To set up,
 all cards are laid face down on the table in a grid.
 Each card has a matching twin,
 so the deck contains pairs of identical images.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On each turn,
 a player flips over two cards of their choice,
 one at a time.
 If the two cards match,
 the player keeps the pair,
 removes them from the table,
 and earns another turn.
 If they do not match,
 the cards are turned back face down in their original positions,
 and play passes to the next player.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key skill is memory:
 players must remember the position of the cards they have seen before,
 even after they are flipped back over,
 so they can later match them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The game continues until all pairs have been collected.
 The winner is the player with the most pairs at the end,
 or the player who collects all pairs when playing alone.
 The game can also be made easier or harder by adding or removing pairs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this version of the game,
 the player that will find the equal card will visualize a pop up image that will explain the quantum state of the ebit;
 to be more educational and to investigate deeply on the quantum entanglement I have created 4 version of the game as experiments to run in a first phase as simulation and then to run it on a real quantum computer.
\end_layout

